  "id": "COMPOUND:757 - PROTEIN:5817",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that etoposide may exert its effects on HepG2 cells through multiple distinct pathways, each involving different molecular targets. The first pathway links etoposide to Leukocyte common antigen, which is then targeted by Mollicellin N, ultimately leading to HepG2 cell activation. The second pathway connects etoposide to 3Beta-Hydroxyolean-12-En-23,29-Dioic Acid, which in turn targets HepG2. The third pathway involves etoposide interacting with P2X purinoceptor 7, which is then targeted by a specific compound, also leading to HepG2 cell response. These findings highlight a complex network of molecular interactions, where etoposide may act through various mechanisms to influence HepG2 cells. The integration of these pathways suggests that etoposide could be a multifaceted agent that modulates cellular processes in HepG2 through different molecular targets, potentially offering diverse therapeutic applications.",
